      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Defined as chronic diarrhea with no identifiable underlying etiology that responds to",
              "pdf_page": 484,
              "printed_page": 402,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "antibiotic therapy.",
              "pdf_page": 484,
              "printed_page": 402,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Antibiotic-responsive diarrhea (ARD) was previously termed idiopathic (primary) small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO); this term is no longer used as it was based on quantitative culture of bacteria in the upper gastrointestinal tract that could not be confirmed by newer PCR-based methods; secondary SIBO is a result of concurrent gastrointestinal diseases (e.g., exocrine pancreatic",
              "pdf_page": 484,
              "printed_page": 402,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "insufficiency [EPI]).",
              "pdf_page": 484,
              "printed_page": 402,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Current theories center on the possibility of immune dysregulation, possibly associated with abnormal CD4+ T-cells, immunoglobulin (Ig) A plasma cells, cytokine expression, and, in German shepherd dogs, mutations in pattern recognition receptors.",
              "pdf_page": 484,
              "printed_page": 402,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
